.UPDATE...
Issued at 850 AM PDT Sun Aug 16 2026

Water vapor imagery shows an upper level trough of low pressure
off the California Coast and high pressure over the Desert
Southwest this morning. As a result, California remains situated
between the two, with south southwesterly flow aloft and a
relatively deep marine layer along the coast, although not as deep
as yesterday. In fact, the 12z KOAK sounding shows the atmosphere
saturated between roughly 600 and 1800 feet. This corresponds
well to the ceilings that we are seeing across our observing sites
as well. Given the depth of the marine layer, we have seen some
drizzle as well this morning, with a number of locations reporting
a hundredth or two. The winner for drizzlefest though is Point
Lobos, Mound Meadow, with 0.03 inches.

This deep marine layer will keep the area quite cool today, with
temperatures staying below normal for the majority of the Bay Area
and near normal for our fartherest inland areas and upper
elevations. Current forecast seems to be handling this well, so no
updates are planned at this time. One other note given that the
marine layer was shallower this morning that yesterday, some of
the upper elevations did not have as good of a humidity recovery.
This nocturnal drying trend will likely continue through the
week, as the high pressure tries to expand into the area. Palmer


