.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1102 PM PDT Sat Aug 15 2026
(Tonight through Monday)

Heights will begin to rise Sunday as upper-level ridging nudges in
from the east. We`re already seeing an effect of this with a
compressing marine layer bringing lower clouds and less inland
extent. Sunday morning will offer another chance for drizzle along
the coast and in the higher terrain. Temperatures will remain below
normal under the marine layer influence with inland and higher
elevation locations warming to near normal. Diurnal winds will keep
smoke from the Timber Fire out to sea with downsloping (easterly)
winds in the morning and onshore (northwesterly) winds in the
afternoon.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1102 PM PDT Sat Aug 15 2026
(Monday night through next Saturday)

Global ensemble clusters are in agreement that upper-level ridging
will nose, and ultimately build, into the Intermountain West through
Thursday. Sensible weather wise, locations inland from and above the
compressed marine layer (~1,000 feet) will moderate to near to
slightly above normal. Along the coast, temperatures will remain
below normal with evening/morning clouds. To put this event into
context, 850 mb temperatures will increase to the 75th-90th
percentile; even so, no records are forecast. Even with the warming
and drying trend, heat and fire weather impacts are expected to
remain minor. Fire weather wise, seasonably elevated conditions with
rapid rates of spread on any wind-and-terrain aligned starts in
receptive vegetation will be possible in higher terrain,
specifically with the diurnal sea breeze in the
afternoons/evenings.

